-- title:
--   The future $\sigma$-algebra is a single pullback along the shift
-- statement:
--   For the coordinate process $Y_i(\omega) = \omega_i$ on the path space $\mathsf{X}^{\mathbb{N}}$, the $\sigma$-algebra generated by the coordinates from time $m$ onwards coincides with the pullback of the full product $\sigma$-algebra along the $m$-shift:
--
--   $$\sigma\bigl(Y_i : i \ge m\bigr) \;=\; \bigl(\text{product } \sigma\text{-algebra}\bigr) \circ \bigl(\omega \mapsto (\omega_{m+n})_{n}\bigr)^{-1}.$$
--
--   **Why this reformulation is the useful one.** The mixing coefficients $\alpha(n)$, $\rho(n)$, $\phi(n)$ are all defined by quantifying over sets $B$ in the *future* $\sigma$-algebra $\sigma(Y_i : i \ge k+n)$, which as written is a supremum of pullbacks along individual coordinate maps — a form that supports no computation at all. This theorem replaces it by a *single* pullback: every future event is $\{\omega : \sigma^m\omega \in B_0\}$ for one measurable subset $B_0$ of path space.
--
--   That is exactly what makes the restart property usable. Knowing that the shifted trajectory has the law of a chain restarted from $P^m(x,\cdot)$ says nothing about a supremum of coordinate pullbacks, but says everything about a single pullback: the probability of a future event becomes the probability of $B_0$ under the restarted chain, and a total-variation rate on $P^m(x,\cdot)$ then bounds it directly. Without this identification the two halves of a mixing estimate — the probabilistic input (restart) and the combinatorial input (the shape of the future $\sigma$-algebra) — cannot be connected.
--
--   **Proof.** The product $\sigma$-algebra is by definition $\bigsqcup_a \mathrm{eval}_a^{-1}$, and pullback commutes with suprema (`comap_iSup`) and with composition (`comap_comp`), so the right-hand side is $\bigsqcup_{a} (\omega \mapsto \omega_{m+a})^{-1}$. It remains to see that this indexed family agrees with $\bigsqcup_{i \ge m}(\omega \mapsto \omega_i)^{-1}$, which is the reindexing bijection $a \mapsto m+a$ between $\mathbb{N}$ and $\{i : i \ge m\}$: one inclusion uses $m \le m+a$, the other writes $i = m + (i-m)$, valid because $m \le i$.
-- source:
--   P. Billingsley, Probability and Measure, 3rd ed., Wiley 1995, Section 36; O. Kallenberg, Foundations of Modern Probability, 2nd ed., Springer 2002, Ch. 1 and Ch. 8; G. L. Jones, "On the Markov Chain Central Limit Theorem", Probability Surveys 1 (2004) 299-320, Section 3 (Definitions 1-3).

import Definitions.Def_MixingCoefficients

open MeasureTheory MeasurableSpace
open MarkovChainCLT

theorem MarkovChainCLT.processSigma_Ici_eq_comap_shift {X : Type*} [MeasurableSpace X] (m : ℕ) :
    processSigma (fun i (ω : ℕ → X) => ω i) (Set.Ici m)
      = MeasurableSpace.comap (fun ω : ℕ → X => fun n => ω (m + n)) inferInstance := by sorry
